arrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Hawaii's presidential race is an open contest, with a term-limited incumbent president leaving both sides without an incumbency advantage. No candidate-specific polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals: a partisan lean of about 13 points toward Democrats, among the strongest in the country.\n\nThat lean has held steadily across recent presidential cycles, and Hawaii rarely draws heavy late-campaign investment from either party, which limits how much a national shift typically moves it. The main uncertainties are the identity of each party's nominee and the 2028 national environment. Early primary polling nationally shows leading figures for both parties, but nothing yet measures a general-election matchup in Hawaii itself. The state's Democratic lean is expected to hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9253, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8703, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9295, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8784, "margin": 13.0}]}